WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.442
This is the one to watch if you only watch
one.

2
00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:09.058
Recording a reading is the job MyWell exists
for, and we are going to do a complete one,

3
00:00:09.178 --> 00:00:12.298
on a practice well, from start to save.

4
00:00:13.117 --> 00:00:15.805
Now we're on: My measurements.

5
00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:20.653
Every reading recorded on your stations lives
here, newest first.

6
00:00:20.773 --> 00:00:25.909
These are Asha's practice readings — and in a
moment we'll record one together,

7
00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:28.885
so you can see exactly how it works.

8
00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:32.381
Add readings — three ways.

9
00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:37.037
Record a reading by hand, pull it straight
from a paired Bluetooth sensor,

10
00:00:37.157 --> 00:00:41.525
or (for admins) upload a whole spreadsheet at
once.

11
00:00:41.645 --> 00:00:45.173
These buttons only appear if you're allowed to
record.

12
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:47.807
Search your readings.

13
00:00:47.927 --> 00:00:50.639
Every reading you've entered lives here.

14
00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:57.263
Search by station name or code, and open the
filters to narrow by station, type or date.

15
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.891
Let's record a reading — together.

16
00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:04.899
We've opened the real recording form on Asha's
open well.

17
00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:10.779
This is a practice run on the tutorial account
— nothing here can touch the real database —

18
00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:13.995
so relax and watch how a reading is made.

19
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:16.593
The reading goes here.

20
00:01:16.713 --> 00:01:22.977
Watch — we've typed today's water level, 11.9
metres, into the highlighted box.

21
00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:27.705
In the field you'd measure with a tape or a
sensor and type it in just like this.

22
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:31.013
More than one reading at a time.

23
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:37.109
Tapping the Water quality chip opens these
fields — pH, TDS and the rest.

24
00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:43.109
One visit can capture the water level, a full
quality check and a photo, all saved together.

25
00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:45.813
One tap to save.

26
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:50.613
That's all a reading takes: a station, a
number, Save.

27
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:54.885
Press Next and we'll press Save for you — then
watch where it lands.

28
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:57.685
And there it is!.

29
00:01:57.805 --> 00:02:02.677
The reading we just made sits at the top of
the list, stamped with today's date.

30
00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:08.125
On your real account, this is the moment your
measurement joins the community's shared dataset.

31
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.150
That reading is now in the list, with your
name and the date on it.

32
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:18.526
Everything you just saw was practice data —
nothing was written to the real database.

33
00:02:18.646 --> 00:02:23.302
You can replay this tutorial any time from the
Learning page inside the app.

34
00:02:23.422 --> 00:02:26.398
Thanks for helping look after our groundwater.
